1. SOURCE AVAILABLE, NOT OPEN SOURCE

   The Enterprise Software is published as source code deliberately. You can
   read it, audit it, learn from it, and satisfy yourself about what it does
   before you run it. That is not the same as an open source license, and this
   document does not become one by being in a public repository.


2. WHAT YOU MAY DO WITHOUT A SUBSCRIPTION

   Without any subscription, payment, or notice to us, you may:

   (a) view, read, and audit the source;
   (b) copy and modify it for the purpose of development, evaluation, testing,
       and security review;
   (c) run it on a development or staging environment that is not used to serve
       real end users; and
   (d) contribute changes back to the project.

   You may do all of the above indefinitely and free of charge.


3. WHAT REQUIRES A SUBSCRIPTION

   You may not use the Enterprise Software in production without a valid Comms
   Enterprise subscription covering the number of seats and hosts in use.

   "Production" means any use that serves real end users or supports the
   operation of a business, whether or not you charge for it, and whether the
   deployment is internal or customer-facing.

   A subscription is granted as a non-exclusive, non-transferable,
   non-sublicensable right to use the Enterprise Software in production for the
   term and scope purchased. It terminates when the subscription lapses.


4. WHAT YOU MAY NOT DO, WITH OR WITHOUT A SUBSCRIPTION

   (a) Remove, disable, bypass, or circumvent the license key verification in
       `src/license.ts` and `src/gate.ts`, or any other technical measure that
       enforces the scope of a subscription; or distribute a modification whose
       purpose or effect is to do so.
   (b) Redistribute, sell, sublicense, rent, or host the Enterprise Software as
       a standalone product or as part of a competing offering.
   (c) Relicense the Enterprise Software, including under the AGPLv3, or
       represent it as being open source.
   (d) Use the Comms name, logo, or brand except to accurately describe the
       origin of the software.

   Section 4(a) does not restrict good-faith security research, nor publishing
   the results of that research.


5. RELATIONSHIP TO THE AGPLv3 PORTION

   The AGPLv3 portion of Comms is a complete, working product on its own. It
   does not depend on the Enterprise Software, and it never requires a license
   key to run. Where the two meet, the Enterprise Software depends on the open
   source portion and not the reverse.

   Combining the open source portion with the Enterprise Software for your own
   production use, under a valid subscription, does not place the Enterprise
   Software under the AGPLv3 and does not oblige you to publish it. The
   copyright holder can license its own work under two sets of terms at once;
   that is what is happening here.

   The AGPLv3's obligations continue to apply, in full, to the AGPLv3 portion.


6. CONTRIBUTIONS

   Contributions to this directory are accepted under CONTRIBUTING.md, which
   includes a copyright license grant permitting distribution under both this
   license and the AGPLv3. Contributors keep the copyright in their own work
   and receive back a perpetual right to use their own contributions.


7. TERM AND TERMINATION

   This license applies for as long as you hold a copy of the Enterprise
   Software. The rights in section 2 are perpetual. The production rights in
   section 3 last only as long as the subscription that granted them.

   Rights under section 3 terminate automatically if you materially breach this
   license and do not cure the breach within 30 days of being notified of it.
   On termination you must stop using the Enterprise Software in production;
   the rights in section 2 survive.


